<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Radziwiłł family</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as House of Radziwiłł</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Radziwiłł family was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> dynasty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>, prominent from the fifteenth century. The family was raised to princes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> in the sixteenth century, and its members held the highest offices of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and vast estates centred on Nesvizh and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slutsk">Slutsk</a>. Barbara Radziwiłł married King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus">Sigismund II Augustus</a>, and the family remained among the richest in Poland-Lithuania.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/radziwill-family.jpg" alt="Radziwiłł family" width="320" height="325" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bastianowa (Bastiana) na podstawie: Juliusz Ostrowski, Księga herbowa rodów polskich. Warszawa 1897 płaszcz - Sodacan · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:POL_COA_Radziwi%C5%82%C5%82_Ksi%C4%85%C5%BC%C4%99_Ia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Radziwiłł family descends from the extinct Lithuanian noble Astikai line. Its first notable member, Kristinas Astikas, a close associate of the Lithuanian ruler Vytautas, became Castellan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>. The name Radvila arose from its use by his son Radvila Astikas and his grandson Mikalojus Radvila the Old. The family adopted a variation of the Trąby coat of arms after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-horodlo">Union of Horodło</a> in 1413.</p><p>Three sons of Mikalojus Radvila the Old, Mikołaj, Jan and Jerzy, became the progenitors of three lines: Goniądz-Meteliai, Biržai-Dubingiai and Nesvizh-Kletsk-Olyka. The first died out in the next generation, since Mikołaj III became a priest and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitia">Samogitia</a> and left no known offspring. The second ended with the death of Ludwika Karolina Radziwiłł in 1695. Only the Nesvizh line survives.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The family joined the state's elite after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-krewo">Union of Krewo</a> in 1385. Its members held high office in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>: 13 were Voivodes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>, seven Grand Chancellors and seven Grand Hetmans. They were the first nobles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> to receive the title of Reichsfürst from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>, a rank held by only five Polish families.</p><p>The family divided on religion. Two branches turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, and Mikołaj the Black financed the Radziwiłł Bible, a Polish Protestant translation published at Brest in 1564. His children converted to Roman Catholicism before the end of the century, while the Biržai line stayed Protestant until it died out.</p><p>The family's importance peaked in the Polish Golden Age. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-deluge">Deluge</a> of the mid-17th century the actions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janusz-radziwill">Janusz Radziwiłł</a> cost it much of its wealth and power. In 1612 the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slutsk">Slutsk</a> passed to the family when Janusz Radziwiłł married the heiress Zofia Olelkowicz Słucka. Nesvizh Castle was awarded to Mikołaj the Black and his brother Jan in 1533. It was sacked in 1706 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a>, Russian forces seized it in 1770 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> expelled the family in 1939.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The family is credited with 23 palaces, 426 towns and 2,032 estates over the centuries. Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoni-radziwill">Antoni Radziwiłł</a>, a composer, turned his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> properties into artistic salons and built the hunting lodge of Antonin between 1822 and 1824 with the architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-friedrich-schinkel">Karl Friedrich Schinkel</a>. Frédéric Chopin stayed there, taught Antoni's daughter Wanda and dedicated his Piano Trio Op. 8 to him.</p><p>The last male Radziwiłł living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, Konstantinas Radvila, was arrested by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a> after the Soviet occupation of 1940 and deported to the Uzbek SSR in 1941, where he died around 1945. The Nesvizh Castle complex, maintained by the family from the 16th century to 1939, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Radziwiłł family” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Deluge of 1655–1660 brought Russian and Swedish invasions, devastation and heavy population losses, and in the eighteenth century the grand duchy, like the whole Commonwealth, fell under Russian influence, its magnate families such as the <b>Radziwiłłs</b> and Sapiehas often more powerful than the state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Through the marriage of the heiress Sophia Olelkovich to Janusz Radziwill, the duchy passed to the <b>Radziwill</b> family, who held it as a private estate.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-slutsk">Duchy of Slutsk</a></li></ul></section>
